WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:06.318
This is Make Your First Beats, by Monobox. Drum machines and rhythm, one step at a time.

00:00:07.118 --> 00:00:10.428
Today: how a drum machine thinks and the kit in a box.

00:00:12.228 --> 00:00:13.227
Before you start.

00:00:14.027 --> 00:00:34.348
Every beat you have ever nodded your head to is built from a few simple parts: a kick, a snare, some hi-hats, and the choice of where to put them in time. A drum machine lets you make those choices one step at a time, hear the result straight away, and change it until it feels right. You do not need to play the drums, or read music, to make a good beat.

00:00:35.148 --> 00:00:50.392
The ebook shows you how a drum machine thinks, what each drum sound does, how the classic rhythms of different genres are built, and how swing, dynamics and sound design turn a stiff pattern into a groove. It ends with how to turn a pattern into a whole track.

00:00:51.892 --> 00:00:52.608
What you need.

00:00:53.408 --> 00:01:00.022
A drum machine or groovebox, in an app or in hardware, and headphones or speakers. That is all.

00:01:01.522 --> 00:01:02.774
How to read the grids.

00:01:03.574 --> 00:01:21.186
The beats in the ebook are written as grids of 16 steps, one bar of four beats. Each row is a sound. An x means the sound plays on that step; a dot means silence. The top row counts the beat the way drummers do: 1 e and a, 2 e and a and so on.

00:01:21.986 --> 00:01:24.040
The example is written out in the ebook.

00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:27.363
Chapter 1 explains this counting in detail.

00:01:28.863 --> 00:01:30.096
How to use the ebook.

00:01:30.896 --> 00:01:40.779
Read one chapter at a time, and program every grid you find in it. Then do the practice section. Beats are learned with your ears and your fingers, not only your eyes.

00:01:41.579 --> 00:01:51.416
Tip: Protect your hearing. Long sessions at high volume, especially on headphones, can damage it for good. Work at a moderate level, and take breaks.

00:01:53.216 --> 00:01:57.325
How a drum machine thinks. Steps, patterns and tempo.

00:01:58.125 --> 00:02:09.977
A drum machine plays sounds at precise moments in time. To do that, it divides time into a grid. Once you understand the grid, every drum machine, app or groovebox will feel familiar.

00:02:11.477 --> 00:02:12.657
Beats and bars.

00:02:13.457 --> 00:02:28.039
Most popular music is counted in groups of four beats. Each group of four is a bar. When you tap your foot to a song, you are usually tapping the beats: 1, 2, 3, 4, 1, 2, 3, 4.

00:02:29.539 --> 00:02:30.238
Steps.

00:02:31.038 --> 00:02:47.547
A step sequencer splits each bar into equal slots called steps. The most common choice is 16 steps per bar: four steps per beat, so each step is a sixteenth note. Drummers count those four steps as 1 e and a (“one, ee, and, a”):

00:02:48.347 --> 00:02:55.125
Steps 1, 5, 9 and 13 fall on the beats: 1, 2, 3, 4.

00:02:55.925 --> 00:03:02.605
Steps 3, 7, 11 and 15 fall halfway between beats: the and, or “and”.

00:03:03.405 --> 00:03:07.026
The other steps are the in-between sixteenths: the e and the a.

00:03:07.826 --> 00:03:30.478
Step sequencing goes back a long way. Roland’s TR-808 Rhythm Composer, introduced in 1980, was programmed with a step sequencer, and its sounds were made with analog circuits, not recordings. Only about 12,000 were made before it was discontinued in 1983, yet it went on to become one of the defining sounds of hip-hop and of electronic dance music.

00:03:31.978 --> 00:03:32.715
Patterns.

00:03:33.515 --> 00:03:45.231
A pattern is one or more bars of steps that loop. You program a pattern, the machine repeats it, and you keep adding and removing steps while it plays. Later you chain patterns together to make a song.

00:03:46.731 --> 00:03:47.298
Tempo.

00:03:48.098 --> 00:03:58.362
Tempo is measured in beats per minute (BPM). At 120 BPM there are two beats every second. A little arithmetic tells you how long each step lasts:

00:03:59.162 --> 00:04:07.510
One beat lasts 60,000 ms divided by the BPM. At 120 BPM, that is 500 ms.

00:04:08.310 --> 00:04:15.216
One step, a sixteenth, is a quarter of that: 125 ms at 120 BPM.

00:04:16.016 --> 00:04:23.532
You do not need the numbers to make beats, but they explain why fast tempos feel busy: the same 16 steps go by in less time.

00:04:24.332 --> 00:04:35.222
Tip: Start each new beat at a medium tempo, around 90 to 120 BPM. It is easier to hear what each step does. You can always change the tempo later.

00:04:36.722 --> 00:04:37.463
Practice.

00:04:38.263 --> 00:04:43.561
First: set your drum machine to 120 BPM, with a 16-step pattern.

00:04:44.361 --> 00:04:52.654
Second: put a kick on steps 1, 5, 9 and 13. Count 1, 2, 3, 4 along with it.

00:04:53.454 --> 00:05:02.223
Third: move the kick to steps 3, 7, 11 and 15. Count and each time it plays. Hear how the feel changes.

00:05:03.023 --> 00:05:09.645
Fourth: change the tempo to 90 BPM and then to 140 BPM, without changing the steps.

00:05:11.445 --> 00:05:14.486
The kit in a box. What each sound does.

00:05:15.286 --> 00:05:23.549
A drum machine is a drum kit you can program. Each sound has a job in the beat. Knowing those jobs helps you decide where to put each one.

00:05:25.049 --> 00:05:25.645
The kick.

00:05:26.445 --> 00:05:40.547
The kick, or bass drum, is the lowest and heaviest sound. It marks the strong points of the beat and carries most of the energy on big speakers. In dance music it often plays on every beat; in hip-hop it plays a more varied rhythm.

00:05:42.047 --> 00:05:43.378
The snare and the clap.

00:05:44.178 --> 00:06:01.160
The snare is a sharp, bright crack. In most popular styles it plays on beats 2 and 4, the backbeat. That is what makes you clap along. The clap, a burst of short hand claps, does the same job with a different colour, and is often layered with the snare or used instead of it.

00:06:02.660 --> 00:06:03.625
The hi-hats.

00:06:04.425 --> 00:06:23.687
The closed hi-hat is a short, tight tick. It usually keeps time, in eighth notes or sixteenth notes, and it sets how busy the beat feels. The open hi-hat is longer and sizzles. On many drum machines, a closed hat cuts off an open one, just as on a real kit, where closing the hat stops it ringing.

00:06:25.187 --> 00:06:27.325
Toms, cymbals and percussion.

00:06:28.125 --> 00:06:32.992
Toms: pitched drums, high, mid and low, mostly used for fills.

00:06:33.792 --> 00:06:37.354
Crash: a loud cymbal that marks the start of a new section.

00:06:38.154 --> 00:06:42.230
Ride: a cymbal for keeping time with a more open sound than the hi-hat.

00:06:43.030 --> 00:06:50.719
Percussion: rim shot, cowbell, shaker, conga, tambourine and more. These add colour and movement.

00:06:52.219 --> 00:06:53.889
High, middle, low.

00:06:54.689 --> 00:07:10.475
A useful way to think about a beat: the kick holds the low end, the snare and clap the middle, and the hats and cymbals the high end. A beat that has something in each range sounds full. If two sounds in the same range play at the same time all the time, they fight each other.

00:07:11.275 --> 00:07:20.205
Tip: Learn your kit before you write beats. Go through every sound on its own, and adjust its level so that no single sound is far louder than the others.

00:07:21.705 --> 00:07:22.446
Practice.

00:07:23.246 --> 00:07:29.408
First: play every sound in your kit, one at a time. Say what job you think each one could do.

00:07:30.208 --> 00:07:36.079
Second: make a pattern with only closed hi-hats on every other step. Then try every step.

00:07:36.879 --> 00:07:41.597
Third: add a snare on steps 5 and 13. That is the backbeat.

00:07:42.397 --> 00:07:46.286
Fourth: swap the snare for a clap, then try both together.

00:07:48.086 --> 00:07:49.175
That's all for today.

00:07:49.975 --> 00:07:51.200
If you want to keep going:

00:07:52.000 --> 00:08:00.935
Monobox is the app behind the ebook. It is a drum machine and groovebox: a place to make beats, step by step, and turn them into grooves.

00:08:01.735 --> 00:08:04.577
Everything the ebook teaches is meant to be tried with it:

00:08:05.377 --> 00:08:07.359
programming patterns on a step grid.

00:08:08.159 --> 00:08:10.291
knowing what each drum sound does.

00:08:11.091 --> 00:08:13.401
building the classic rhythms of each genre.

00:08:14.201 --> 00:08:17.091
adding swing, dynamics and sound design.

00:08:17.891 --> 00:08:19.824
turning a pattern into a whole track.

00:08:20.624 --> 00:08:25.155
This ebook is free. Share it with anyone who wants to make their first beats.

00:08:25.955 --> 00:08:29.929
This episode of Make Your First Beats was narrated with a synthetic voice.

